{
  "requirement": "```rust,ignore\nimpl Sources {\n    // ...\n\n    /// List file identities within this scope.\n    pub async fn list(&self, scope: &SourceScope) -> Result<Vec<PathBuf>, SourceError>;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement asks for one signature: `impl Sources { pub async fn list(&self, scope: &SourceScope) -> Result<Vec<PathBuf>, SourceError> }`.\n\n- **Signature:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[list]` is a `pub async fn` in `impl Sources`. It takes `&self` and `scope: &SourceScope`, and returns `Result<Vec<PathBuf>, SourceError>`. This matches the requirement exactly.\n- **Types:** `Sources`, `SourceScope` and `SourceError` are all defined in `public.rs` (`struct[Sources]`, `struct[SourceScope]`, `enum[SourceError]`).\n- **Delegation:** `list` calls `self.analysis.provider.list(scope).await?`. The trait method `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#trait[SourceProvider]/fn[list]` returns `Result<Vec<ResourceId>, Self::Error>`. The trait bounds `Error: From<SourceError>`, and `LocalSources` sets `type Error = SourceError`. So the `?` yields `SourceError` and type-checks.\n- **Result conversion:** `list` maps each `ResourceId` to `PathBuf::from(id.location)`, which gives the required `Vec<PathBuf>`.\n- **Trait method:** `SourceProvider::list` is the interface the public method delegates through. It is marked `{impl}` for this requirement and contributes the scope-listing contract.\n\nI found no structural gaps. The requirement text states no behavior beyond the signature, and there are no acknowledgments or refining declarations.",
  "references": "d9046555e6a66d74d87b360f0d21d33a944acf8102331aeceda8f1bc9cd1ff67",
